WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.580
To celebrate the release of Mad Max: Fury
Road, let’s take a look at 12 things you

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.450
probably didn’t know about the post-apocalyptic
sequel.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.670
Fury Road didn’t start life with a traditional
screenplay.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:19.340
Instead, writer and director George Miller
created a visual story with comic book artist

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.189
Brendan McCarthy, who, after seeing Mad Max
2 in the 80s, co-created the comic Freakwave,

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:28.350
which he’s described as ‘Mad Max goes
surfing’!

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:33.090
With the help of storyboard artists, Peter
Pound and Mark Sexton, Miller and McCarthy

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:39.809
created the movie’s visual first draft,
a storyboard with 3,500 panels, which covered

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:46.659
the conference room walls of Miller’s studio
and helped him film and edit the movie shot-by-shot.

00:00:46.659 --> 00:00:51.680
Before production started, George Miller created
an online resource about the world of Fury

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.070
Road for the movie’s cast and crew.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.780
The secure website included videos of stunt
tests, costume fittings, character backstories,

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.519
and the histories and hierarchies of each
of the movie’s tribes.

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:09.040
In the post-apocalyptic world of Fury Road,
everything has to be reused and repurposed.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.150
So, to make sure they got the right look for
the movie’s props, the production design

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.000
team used real salvage material.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.920
For example, they used soda cans, tyres and
inner tubes to make weapons; melted-down old

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:27.750
tankards and trophies to make car accessories;
and 350 salvaged cars to part-build and customise

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.820
a host of vehicles.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:35.810
Furiosa’s mechanical arm was designed and
built by industrial designer Matt Boug, who

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.890
used salvage materials such as spanners, parts
of car engines, and a motor from a toy airplane.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.870
For added mobility, a lighter version of the
arm was also produced for Charlize Theron

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.640
and her stunt double.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:54.690
A core team of 65 stunt performers brought
to life Immortan Joe’s army of War Boys.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:02:00.010
To really get into their cultish characters,
the stunt performers took part in drama workshops

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:07.140
with actor and Fury Road co-writer Nico Lathouris,
as well as doing intense fight training.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.300
George Miller got the idea for the War Boys’
aerial attack team, the Polecats, after seeing

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.640
street performers balancing on poles.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.470
Former Cirque du Soleil performer Stephen
Bland helped put together a team of gymnasts

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.280
to play the Polecats.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.810
Once up in the air, the Polecats got their
cues via earpieces and laser pointers on the

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:27.810
vehicles.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:32.480
High-tensile steel, which is strong but has
some flexibility, was used for the poles.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.260
And each 30-foot pole was counterweighted
with an engine block at the base, which was

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.790
pushed or pulled by the vehicle’s stunt
team, allowing the Polecats to glide through

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.870
the air.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:49.470
Australian musician iOTA, who plays the Doof
Warrior, wrote and recorded his own war tunes

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.160
which he plays on a double-necked electric
guitar as the frontman of the crazy band whose

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.460
music rallies Immortan Joe’s troops.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.349
To help the band’s Taiko drummers keep their
tempo, amid the noise of engines and combat,

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:05.569
the music was piped to them via earpieces.

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:10.480
To get a sense of their personalities, George
Miller asked actresses auditioning for Immortan

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.620
Joe’s Five Wives to read a scene from any
film or TV show except Fury Road.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.850
Choices included a Monty Python scene, which
was picked by first-time-actress Abbey Lee.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:26.000
To prepare to play the Five Wives, Lee and
fellow actors Rosie Huntington-Whiteley, Riley

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.500
Keough, Zoe Kravitz and Courtney Eaton, spent
three weeks in Sydney rehearsing and working

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.379
with Fury Road co-writer Nico Lathouris and
choreographer Meryl Tankard.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:40.800
As background for their characters, they also
researched violence against women in war zones

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.480
with Vagina Monologues playwright, Eve Ensler,
who’s worked with female rape victims in

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.870
the Congo.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:52.981
At the peak of filming, there were over 1,700
crew members working on Fury Road, and an

00:03:52.981 --> 00:03:55.910
average of 1,000 people on set at any time.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:01.370
The production’s base camp was the size
of three football fields and, during 120 days,

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.769
moved six times.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:08.000
To move gear from one location to the next,
they needed five 8 x 8 former German military

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.580
transport trucks.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:16.070
To create Fury Road’s Wasteland, the filmmakers
needed a location that was flat, had sand

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.370
and canyons, and little vegetation.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.550
Originally, they wanted to return to Broken
Hill in the Australian Outback, where they

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:27.740
shot Mad Max 2, but two years of high rainfall
meant the area was too green.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.789
However, Namibia in southern Africa had everything
they needed, location-wise, including the

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:37.810
coastal city of Swakopmund, which provided
the production hub, and the Namib Desert,

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.960
which had the range of looks required for
the Road War.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.689
Fury Road’s Citadel was actually a series
of sets constructed in Namibia, Sydney, and

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:50.439
Cape Town, and its base was built of sand
and fake stone.

00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:55.080
However, the Citadel’s towers and surrounding
views were computer generated.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.949
The rock walls were made using high-res photos
taken of Australia’s Blue Mountains, which

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:04.710
were then digitally cut and bent to form the
shape of the Citadel.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:09.680
Australian Motocross Champ and freestyle coach
Stephen Gall helped put together a team of

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.749
expert freestylers to play the Rock Riders
who prowl the canyon.

00:05:13.749 --> 00:05:18.110
A series of ramps and rollercoasters were
built to aid their movement around the steep

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.500
canyon walls.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.009
Filming took place at Namibia’s Swakop River
Valley and the size of the canyon was increased

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:25.009
with CGI.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:29.729
Well, there you have it, 12 things you probably
didn't know about Mad Max: Fury Road!

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:33.979
Now, let me know in the comments below, what
did you think of the movie?

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:36.339
And what would you like to see happen in a
sequel?

00:05:36.339 --> 00:05:40.839
